      "headline": "Numerica Credit Union awards $380,000 to regional nonprofits",
      "subhead": "Through the Numerica Charitable Fund, members help provide food, housing, and education support for local families.",
      "sponsor_line": "Sponsored content from Numerica Credit Union",
      "body": "$380,000 was distributed to regional nonprofits in August. Numerica Credit Union provided these funds through its 2025 Charitable Fund.\n\nThe Numerica Charitable Fund supports organizations that align with the Numerica CARES for Kids initiative. This program focuses on addressing youth poverty by providing resources to children and families in Eastern and Central Washington and North Idaho.\n\nThe funding is generated by the Skip a Pay program. Numerica members pay a $30 fee to skip a monthly loan payment, and those fees are contributed to the Charitable Fund. This year, the total investment grew by more than 40% compared to the $372,000 distributed in the previous cycle.\n\nThese grants pay for critical community needs. Supported services include nutritious meals, safe housing, school supplies, and trauma-informed care. The credit union fully funded every selected grant request to ensure organizations have the resources they need.\n\nLong-term support is a core part of the fund's strategy. Four organizations are currently in the third year of receiving $100,000 multi-year grants. These partners include Family Promise of Spokane, Safe Passage in North Idaho, the Women's Resource Center of North Central Washington in Wenatchee, and Communities In Schools of Benton-Franklin in the Tri-Cities.\n\n\"You can imagine how thrilled we are to have our friends at Numerica by our side to make sure that children and their families have the food they need, Numerica is such a shining example of the good in the world, supporting not just Second Harvest but also other amazing nonprofits,\" says Jason Clark, CEO of Second Harvest. \"We're so grateful for Numerica's kindness and can't wait to see the positive impact we'll make together.\"\n\nCarla Cicero, Numerica president and CEO, says the credit union wanted to provide as much relief as possible. \"The need is greater than ever, More families are struggling, more children need support, and many nonprofits are being asked to do more with less,\" Cicero says. \"We wanted to provide as much relief as possible by fully funding every selected grant request, giving these organizations the resources they need to continue changing lives.\"",
